WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.333
ROC AUC.

2
00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:06.092
Every metric so far depended on one chosen threshold.

3
00:00:06.092 --> 00:00:07.267
This one does not.

4
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:09.567
Here is the idea.

5
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:15.133
Sweep the cutoff, and at each point plot the true positive rate against the false positive rate.

6
00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:17.338
That trace is the ROC curve.

7
00:00:17.338 --> 00:00:20.400
The area under it summarizes ranking quality.

8
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.267
Here is how the curve is built.

9
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.067
Start with a cutoff of one, flagging nothing.

10
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:28.533
Lower it step by step, flagging more.

11
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:31.933
At each step, measure the true and false positive rates.

12
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:34.533
Each cutoff adds one point to the plot.

13
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:37.200
Now compare two models.

14
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.567
The dashed diagonal is a coin flip.

15
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.772
The curve hugging the top left corner wins.

16
00:00:42.772 --> 00:00:44.867
Here, XGBoost beats the forest.

17
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.967
AUC has a lovely meaning.

18
00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:55.000
It is the probability that a randomly chosen positive gets a higher score than a randomly chosen negative.

19
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.467
Point five is a coin flip, and one is perfect ranking.

20
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.567
In code, we loop over the models.

21
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:04.900
We use predicted probabilities, not hard labels.

22
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.067
Then we get the curve and the area, and plot each model.

23
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:10.200
One caution.

24
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:18.583
When negatives hugely outnumber positives, the false positive rate barely moves, so ROC AUC can look rosy.

25
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:22.167
Precision recall AUC is often more informative.

26
00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:24.800
Here is the real use.

27
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.533
Your screen and your ranker will run at very different thresholds.

28
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:32.833
So you pick the model with AUC, which ignores thresholds.

29
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:37.533
XGBoost scores point nine three, against point nine for the forest.

30
00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:40.081
Choose any operating point later.

31
00:01:40.081 --> 00:01:42.533
XGBoost ranks better at all of them.

32
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:44.646
Let's recap.

33
00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:46.700
ROC sweeps every threshold.

34
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.400
AUC is the chance a positive outranks a negative.

35
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.033
On extreme imbalance, try precision recall AUC.

36
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.367
Next up: the confusion matrix.
